  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1153 PM MDT Tue Oct 6 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nUpper-level ridging in the region will continue to dominate through \nFriday, which will continue the warm and dry pattern through then. \nTemperatures will remain well above normal through Friday, with \ntemperatures peaking again on Friday. Starting Friday, the next \nincoming trough will begin to drive south out of Canada, increasing \nwest to southwest flow aloft. The ridge breakdown will also bring in \na mid level jet. This jet coinciding with daytime heating will mix \nthese winds down to the surface. As a result breezy to gusty winds \nare forecasted for Friday.\n\nThe trough and with the cold front starts to push south into \nMontana by early Saturday, bringing the start to decreasing \ntemperatures and precipitation. The trough continues to dig south\nthroughout the weekend and into Monday, which will keep the \ncooling trend with mountain snow and rain/snow at lower \nelevations. The coldest lows will be Monday morning. There remain \nmodel variability in timing and snow amounts still.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nConfidence is increasing for cold temperatures and mountain snow \nSaturday through Monday. Models have snow levels falling behind the \ncold front throughout the day Saturday, with the best push for \ncolder air aloft moving through Saturday night into Sunday \nmorning. There will be a good drop in temperatures (up to 20 to 30\ndegrees) Friday with lower elevation highs forecasted in the 30s \nto 40s Sunday.\n\nModels have been trending towards a splitting trough solution in \nthe most recent runs. This has the trough digging and splitting \noff the cut off low farther to the south by California. This trend\nas a result, has been decreasing the snow amounts a little bit, \nkeeping most of the snow in the mountains. With lower elevations \nforecasted to drop below freezing Saturday night into Sunday \nmorning, that will allow for some snow to fall. There remains a \n40-70% chance for 2\" of snow to fall across the Southwest Valleys,\nthe Highway 200 corridor, and the Rocky Mountain Front. With this\nbeing a marginal temperature snow event, there is a question on \nhow much snow accumulates at first with an initially warm \nground/road pavement. \n\nFor the mountains, models have been trending the North-Central \nContinental Divide and the Little/Big Belts to receive the \nhighest amounts. Lee troughing behind the front help keep snow \nthere as well as the front stalling by Southwest MT mountains \nSaturday, allowing a convergence zone to set up. There remains \nsome uncertainty in how much snow falls along these zones but \nthere is a 40-70% chance for 9\" of snow for Kings Hill, Rogers and\nMarias Pass. There may be some melting of snow on these passes \nbefore moderate snow rates crash surface temperatures. The snow \nwill be wet and heavier, which can also weigh down power \nlines/trees. \n\nI would like to see some more consistency before hopping onto a \nsolution so we will continue to monitor on how things trend leading \nto the event. -Wilson",
